Hello,

I am seeing a suspend/resume regression with i915 on a Lenovo ThinkPad P1 G=
en 7 for anything 7.0+. 6.18 LTS is fine.

Attaching the drm.debug=3D0x1e dmesg: i915-fail-kernel_7.1.0_1-2.log.xz

The problem happens when the laptop is suspended by closing the lid and rem=
ains suspended for more than a few minutes. Deterministically. On resume, t=
he machine does not recover the internal display properly. Short lid-close =
sleeps, for example only a couple of seconds, appear to resume correctly.

The affected display is the internal eDP panel. Same behavior with external=
 panel though.

Hardware:
* Lenovo ThinkPad P1 Gen 7 (Intel 165H, MTL)
* Product: 21KV001UCK
* BIOS: LENOVO N48ET34W (1.21), 2026-05-11
* GPU: Intel Meteor Lake-P / Intel Arc Graphics [8086:7d55] rev 08
* Subsystem: Lenovo [17aa:2235]
* Kernel driver in use: i915
* xe module is present but not used

Environment:
* Void Linux
* Kernel: 7.1.0
* Xfce on Xorg
* Internal eDP panel only
* Kernel command line included:
  drm.debug=3D0x1e log_buf_len=3D32M

Reproduction:
1. Boot the system with i915.
2. Start Xfce/Xorg.
3. Close the laptop lid and let it suspend.
4. Leave it suspended for more than a few minutes.
5. Open the lid / resume the laptop.

Expected result:
The internal eDP panel resumes normally.

Actual result:
After a longer suspend, the laptop does not recover the internal display pr=
operly. Very short suspend/resume cycles, for example only a couple of seco=
nds, work correctly. SSH works, but shutdown crashes the entire laptop.

I am attaching the dmesg log captured with drm.debug=3D0x1e and log_buf_len=
=3D32M.

Please let me know if you need any additional debug information or if there=
 is a specific i915 parameter you would like me to test. Already tried i915=
.enable_psr=3D0 and i915.enable_fbc=3D0 with no change.

I have seen similar reports around i915 suspend/resume behavior on 7.0+ ker=
nels for ThinkPad P1 Gen 7 / MTL systems, so this may not be an isolated ca=
se. Sorry if this is already known.

Best,
Jakub

